body:
When program execution reaches a omp sections directive, program segments defined by the following omp section directive are distributed for parallel execution among available threads. Is the original thread, i.e. the thread which runs before reaching omp sections , one of the threads that execute one of the program segments defined by the omp section directives? after all the threads finish executing their program segments defined by omp section directives, will the original thread be the only one which remains existing and continue execution? In the following example, which thread is the original thread, 1 or 2 or something else? #pragma omp sections
{

    # pragma omp section
    { printf("thread 1 here\n"); }
    # pragma omp section
    { printf("thread 2 here\n"); }

} Thanks.

answers.body:
Your code example is incomplete. Let's complete it naively. int main()
{
    #pragma omp sections
    {
        #pragma omp section
        { printf("thread 1 here\n"); }
        # pragma omp section
        {printf("thread 2 here\n"); }
    }
} There is only a single thread here. #pragma omp section alone creates no additional threads. You use sections within a parallel construct: #pragma omp parallel
{
    #pragma omp sections Which is equivalent to #pragma omp parallel sections The parallel construct creates a team of threads. All threads run until the sections construct, wherein each section block will be executed by one of the threads in the team. Talking about threads entering / leaving a parallel section: Yes, the thread that encounters the parallel construct will become part of the team running. In fact it will become it's master thread with the thread number 0 . It will participate in the execution of sections if the team runs into a sections construct. It is however not guaranteed that it will execute any section, there may not even be enough sections for all threads. The scheduling method from section to thread within a team is implementation defined. Yes, after the end of a parallel region, the original thread is the (only) one that will continue the execution of the outer code.

