Apparently there are more instructions in rand_r() compared to rand(). Below is copied from one implementation. So it's reasonable that rand_r() takes more time to complete one round than rand(). int
rand_r(unsigned int *ctx)
{
    u_long val = (u_long) *ctx;
    int r = do_rand(&val);

    *ctx = (unsigned int) val;
    return (r);
}


static u_long next = 1;

int
rand()
{
    return (do_rand(&next));
} And since rand() is not thread safe, the output could be incorrect if you use rand() in parallel. The worse part is that you would still get a result and don't know if it's correct in small scale test.

