answers.body:
The problem you're observing is due to the variable visibility. I have rerun your application while compiling it for gcc 4.8.4 and icc 16.0.3 and I got the following results (which vary across executions): gcc Thread ID: 2, task: 1
Thread ID: 2, task: 5
Thread ID: 2, task: 5
Thread ID: 1, task: 5
Thread ID: 0, task: 2 icc Thread ID: 0, task: 5
Thread ID: 1, task: 3
Thread ID: 1, task: 5
Thread ID: 0, task: 5
Thread ID: 2, task: 5 Since visibility of variable i is not declared in the #pragma omp construct the compiler decides that i should be declared as shared , which means that modifications done by any thread are observed by the remaining threads. In your case, since you expect messages being printed showing task 0 to 4, that means that each thread should have its private copy of i , as in the following code -- which derives from yours. Notice the modification in the #pragma omp parallel : #include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <omp.h>

void task(int p)
{
  printf("Thread ID: %d, task: %d\n", omp_get_thread_num(), p);
}

#define N       5
int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
  int i;
  #pragma omp parallel num_threads(3) private(i)
  {
    #pragma omp single
    {
      for(i = 0;i < N; i++)
      {
        #pragma omp task
        task(i);
      }
    }
  }
  return 0;
} The result of this version, no matter the compiler you use, is: Thread ID: 2, task: 1
Thread ID: 2, task: 3
Thread ID: 2, task: 4
Thread ID: 1, task: 0
Thread ID: 0, task: 2 NOTE that the placement of private can either go into the #pragma omp parallel construct, or as user smateo suggests, it can go into the #pragma omp task (but in the latter case as firstprivate to keep the value of the variable when going into the task construct). The actual decision on where it goes depends on the semantics of your application.

