With MPI you would do something like: int rank, size;

MPI_Init();
MPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &rank);
MPI_Comm_size(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &size);

int start = (rank*N)/size;
int end = ((rank+1)*N)/size;

for (i = start; i < end; i++)
{
   sprintf(cmd, "./openmp_parallelized_program %f %f", a[i], b[i]);
   system(cmd);
}

MPI_Finalize(); Then run the MPI job with one process per node. There is a caveat though. Some MPI implementations do not allow processes to call fork() under certain conditions (and system() calls fork() ), e.g. if they communicate over RDMA-based networks like InfiniBand. Instead, you could merge both programs in order to create one hybrid MPI/OpenMP program.

