answers.body:
A variable that appears in a reduction clause must not appear in other clauses such as private , shared , etc... The compiler does what is needed to perform the reduction, including a hidden privatisation of the variable. You may be then tempted to write reduction(+:array_101x101) , but by doing so you wouldn't ask for a reduction on the elements of the array, but on the pointer itself (and the compiler won't let you do that). In C/C++ there's no direct support for OpenMP reductions on arrays (as opposed to Fortran) . Victor Eijkhout as suggested an approach with a class, there is another one that consists in performing the reduction manually: #pragma omp parallel 
{
    long int* private_array = new long int[101*101];
    for (int i=0; i<101*101; i++) private_array[i] = 0;

    #pragma omp for
    for (int i = 0; i < i_length; i++) {
        private_array[input_array1[i]*101 + input_array2[i]]++;
    }

    #pragma omp critical
    for (int j=0; j<101; j++) {
        for (int i=0; j<101; j++) {
            array_101x101[j][i] = array_101x101[j][i] + private_array[j*101 + i];
        }
    }

    delete private_array;
} It will be less performant than an automatic reduction, though. EDIT @Paleonix reminded me in the comments that there is actually some support for array reduction, at least for 1D arrays. I makes things simpler, but you'd better declare your array as 1D from the start: long int* array_101x101 = new long int[101*101];
for (int i=0; i<101*101; i++) array_101x101[i] = 0;
#pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:array_101x101[:101*101])
{
    for (int i = 0; i < i_length; i++) {
        array_101x101[input_array1[i]*101 + input_array2[i]]++;
    }
}

